[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Container Hardening: Kernel Capabilities and Seccomp/AppArmor in Docker"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, Docker containers share the host's Linux kernel. While this provides high performance and efficiency, it also introduces security risks if a container breaks out or is compromised. To mitigate these risks, Docker relies on three core Linux security mechanisms: Kernel Capabilities, Seccomp (Secure Computing Mode), and AppArmor. This tutorial explores how these components work and how to configure them for production environments."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Linux Kernel Capabilities"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditionally, the Linux privilege model is binary: either a process has root privileges (UID 0), with unrestricted access to the system, or it is an unprivileged user. Linux capabilities break down root privileges into distinct, granular units. For example, instead of giving a process full root access, you can grant it only the capability to bind to low-numbered ports (CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE) or change system time (CAP_SYS_TIME)."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you run a standard Docker container, Docker drops a large subset of default capabilities to reduce the attack surface. You can view or modify these capabilities using the --cap-add and --cap-drop flags."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"docker run --rm --cap-drop=ALL --cap-add=NET_ADMIN nginx"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"--cap-drop=ALL removes all default capabilities from the container."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"--cap-add=NET_ADMIN adds back specifically the network administration capability."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Limiting System Calls with Seccomp"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Seccomp is a Linux kernel feature that restricts the system calls (syscalls) that a process can make. A syscall is the fundamental interface between a user-space application and the kernel. By filtering syscalls, you prevent malicious code inside a container from interacting with the underlying kernel, even if the application runs as root."},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Docker provides a default Seccomp profile that blocks over 40 dangerous or obsolete syscalls out of a total of more than 300. You can apply a custom Seccomp profile using the --security-opt flag."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"docker run --rm --security-opt seccomp=/path/to/custom-profile.json alpine sh"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is a basic example of a Seccomp JSON profile that denies the 'reboot' syscall:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"{
  "defaultAction": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW",
  "architectures": [
    "SCMP_ARCH_X86_64",
    "SCMP_ARCH_X86",
    "SCMP_ARCH_X32"
  ],
  "syscalls": [
    {
      "name": "reboot",
      "action": "SCMP_ACT_ERRNO",
      "args": []
    }
  ]
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Enforcing Mandatory Access Control with AppArmor"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"AppArmor is a Linux Security Module (LSM) that allows the system administrator to restrict programs' capabilities with per-program profiles. These profiles can allow or forbid operations such as file reading, writing, network binding, and execution."},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Docker automatically applies a default AppArmor profile named 'docker-default' to all containers. This profile places containers into a restrictive confinement, blocking writes to sensitive host directories like /proc and /sys."},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To run a container with an unconfined AppArmor profile (not recommended for production) or a custom profile, use the security-opt flag:"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"docker run --rm --security-opt apparmor=unconfined alpine sh"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Principle of Least Privilege: Never run containers with unnecessary capabilities. Drop all capabilities using --cap-add=ALL and add back only what is required."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage Defaults: Rely on Docker's default Seccomp and AppArmor profiles unless your application specifically requires custom modifications."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid --privileged: The --privileged flag disables all security mechanisms (capabilities, seccomp, apparmor), effectively giving the container root access to the host. Use granular flags instead."}]
